# Coding defaults (minimal change + UI craft)

## Always (every code change)

Prefer the **smallest complete** change. No unrequested abstractions, wrappers, options, config, sample code, or new dependencies.

Ladder (take the first that fully solves it):
1. Skip features/options nobody asked for.
2. Reuse existing helpers / components / patterns in this repo.
3. Prefer stdlib or platform APIs; prefer project wrappers over raw primitives when the project has them.
4. Use already-installed deps only.
5. One readable line if that works.
6. Otherwise the least code that works.

Hard rules:
- Lazy on scope, never lazy on completeness: update every necessary call site, test, fixture, and export.
- Keep existing layering, interfaces, and conventions. Delete beats add.
- Comments only for why the code cannot say; one line.
- Bugfix: grep every caller of changed functions; fix the root cause once in shared code.
- Preserve error handling and validation when moving/merging code.
- Non-trivial new logic gets a small test or assert-based self-check; trivial edits do not.
- Shortcuts: comment `shortcut: <limit>, <when to upgrade>`.

**Never drop:** trust-boundary validation, data-loss error handling, security, accessibility, hardware calibration the product needs, or anything the user explicitly asked for.

PR / change report must end with: what you skipped or did not check; risks the user must know (or "no extra risk").

## Review (when reviewing a diff / PR)

Order: correct → safe → scales → tested → fast → lean.

Report only findings with a concrete case ("this input → this wrong result"). Suggest the smallest useful fix; prefer deleting code.

Format:
- What this change does: (2–3 sentences)
- Must fix / Should fix / Nice to have (skip empty groups; continuous numbering)
- Each item: What this is / Problem / Fix / If we skip it
- Verdict: … ; Lean: -N lines possible (if any); Not checked: … (if any)
- Or: Looks good. Ship. + what you checked

## Frontend / UI only

When editing UI (web, landing, dashboard, components, forms, native screens):

- Read PRODUCT.md / DESIGN.md if present; do not invent missing product facts.
- Prefer refinement over redesign unless asked to redesign.
- After direction is set, before UI edits: honor craft floor below.
- Verify on a real render (batch checks, not one screenshot per tweak).
- Theme browser chrome from the palette: selection, caret, scrollbar, focus ring, underline offset, tabular numerals.
- Copy: product language; controls name the action; errors name problem + recovery.

### Craft floor — verify
- Contrast: body/placeholder ≥4.5:1; large text ≥3:1; tint secondary text from surface hue, never gray on color.
- Depth: shadows need offset + soft blur; zero-offset colored halo is decoration.
- Spacing: tight groups, generous separation; more space above a heading than below.
- Type: body ~65–75ch; display max 6rem; tracking floor -0.04em; clear scale/weight steps; fix overflow at breakpoints.
- Motion: one authored moment, not scatter; ease-out from already-visible default.
- States: hover, disabled, loading, error, empty; keyboard focus; real content.

### Craft floor — refuse (unless brief earns it)
- Same-size icon+heading+text card grids as page structure; nested cards always wrong.
- Hero-metric template (big number + label + supporting stats).
- **Ban:** kicker/eyebrow above a heading — delete it; heading stands alone.
- Section numbers 01/02/03 unless sequence carries needed meaning.
- Modal for tasks that need neither interruption nor protected focus.
- Gradient text; decorative glass/blur; >1px colored side borders on cards/callouts.
- Hard offset neobrutalist shadows outside a neobrutalist world.
- Sparklines/progress rings/soft rounded boxes as content stand-ins.
- Monospace as "technical" costume (ok for code/data/measurement).
- System display faces as the voice of an own-world page — source a face that matches.
- Unicode/emoji as icon system; geometric masks faking organic photo edges.
- Light/dark chosen by category habit instead of use scene.

The floor never picks the visual world. When torn between refined and committed, commit.
